  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most disappointing outdoor lighting fails for predictable reasons. Fixtures are too bright, too blue, too evenly spaced, or aimed into the neighbor's bedroom window. The landscape lighting design guidelines below are the working rules that professional designers apply on every project, adapted for the live oaks, limestone, and Hill Country terrain that define Austin properties. They are not rigid laws, but when a yard looks effortlessly good at night, you can usually trace it back to these principles. Austin Landscape Lighting has applied them across more than 500 installations, and this guide shares them so you can evaluate any design, whether you build it yourself or hire it out.</p>

<h2>Guideline 1: Light the Destination, Not the Fixture</h2>
<p>The most important rule is also the one most often broken. The eye should land on an illuminated tree trunk, a textured stone wall, or a safe walking surface, never on a bright glowing fixture. In practice this means choosing fixtures with deep shrouds and glare guards, aiming beams away from sightlines, and favoring concealed placement behind shrubs, under eaves, or inside tree canopies.</p>
<p>When you walk a finished project, the test is simple. Stand at the street, the front door, and the main patio seat. If you can see any LED source directly, that fixture needs a longer shroud, a lower aim, or a new position.</p>

<h2>Guideline 2: Standardize on 2700K Warm White</h2>
<p>Color temperature sets the mood of the whole property, and consistency matters more than the exact number. For residential work, 2700K warm white is the default. It flatters Austin stone, cedar, brick, and foliage, and it reads as inviting rather than clinical. A 3000K lamp is acceptable for modern architecture with gray stucco or steel, and some designers use 4000K sparingly on blue-green plants like agave or Texas sage to make them pop, but mixing temperatures without intent makes a yard look patched together.</p>
<p>Avoid 5000K and above for anything residential. That is the color of a parking lot. Our <a href="/tools/color-temperature-guide/">color temperature guide</a> shows side-by-side comparisons on common Austin materials.</p>

<h2>Guideline 3: Match Beam Spread to the Target</h2>
<p>Low-voltage LED lamps come in standard spreads, and selecting the right one is half of good aiming. The ranges below are a reliable starting point.</p>
<h3>15 degree spot</h3>
<p>Tall, narrow targets. A single cedar elm trunk, a columnar cypress, a flagpole, or a chimney. The tight beam carries 30 to 40 feet before it loses punch.</p>
<h3>30 to 38 degree flood</h3>
<p>The workhorse for most uplighting. A mature live oak trunk with a spreading canopy, a two-story facade column, a specimen yaupon. Enough spread to cover the trunk, enough intensity to reach the first branches.</p>
<h3>45 to 60 degree wide flood</h3>
<p>Broad surfaces. Washing a limestone wall from 4 to 6 feet out, lighting a wide hedge, or filling a garden bed with soft ambient light. Lower intensity per square foot but even coverage.</p>
<p>A useful habit is to buy fixtures that accept interchangeable MR16 or similar lamps so spreads can be changed after the first night test without digging anything up.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscape-lighting-design-guidelines-2.webp" alt="Diagram-style view of uplights at different beam spreads aimed at a tree trunk and a stone wall" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>A 15 degree spot reaches high into a tall trunk, while a 60 degree wide flood evens out light across a textured stone wall.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Guideline 4: Layer Three Types of Light</h2>
<p>A yard with only one layer of light looks flat.</p>
<p>Accent light is the drama: uplights on trees, grazing on stone, silhouetting a specimen plant against a wall. Task light is the function: path lights, step lights, and downlights over grills and doors. Ambient light is the glue: soft moonlighting from high in a tree canopy, a low wash on a hedge, or a dim glow across a lawn that keeps the dark areas from feeling like holes.</p>
<p>A common mistake in Austin yards is loading up on accent uplights because they are satisfying, then wondering why the property feels like a stage set. Adding two or three moonlighting fixtures 20 to 30 feet up in a live oak, aimed down through the branches with a 60 degree spread, fills the space with dappled light and ties the composition together.</p>

<h2>Guideline 5: Space Path Lights for Pools of Light, Not Runways</h2>
<p>Path lights should be placed every 6 to 8 feet, staggered on alternate sides of the walk, and set 12 to 18 inches back from the edge so the hat of the fixture is not kicked or run over by a mower. Fixtures placed in a straight line at equal intervals on one side create the airport runway look that gives landscape lighting a bad name. Fewer, well-placed fixtures that overlap at the edges of their light pools almost always read better than more.</p>
<p>Fixture height matters too. An 18 to 22 inch path light suits most residential walks. In beds with Texas sage or dwarf yaupon that grow 2 to 3 feet, plan for the plants to engulf short fixtures within two seasons. For step-by-step spacing on your own walk, see <a href="/how-many-landscape-lights-do-i-need/">how many landscape lights do I need</a>.</p>

<h2>Guideline 6: Respect Voltage Drop and Transformer Headroom</h2>
<p>A 12V system loses voltage along the cable. If a fixture at the end of a 150-foot run receives 9V, it will be visibly dimmer than one near the transformer, even with LEDs that tolerate a range. The rules that keep this in check are straightforward.</p>
<p>Size the transformer so the total fixture wattage uses no more than 80 percent of its rating. That 20 percent headroom covers inrush, future additions, and heat. A system with 14 fixtures at 5 watts each totals 70 watts, so a 100 watt transformer is appropriate rather than a 75 watt unit at its limit.</p>
<p>Keep each cable run under about 100 feet on 12-gauge wire for loads up to 100 watts, or step up to 10-gauge for longer runs. Use a hub or T-method layout so fixtures branch from a central point instead of daisy-chaining in a long line. On multi-tap transformers, use the 13V or 14V tap for distant runs so fixtures receive close to 12V after the drop. Our <a href="/tools/transformer-calculator/">transformer calculator</a> works through these numbers for a specific layout.</p>

