<h2>Guideline 7: Choose Materials for a Twenty-Year Life</h2>
<p>Austin summers are hard on hardware. Powder-coated aluminum fixtures chalk, fade, and eventually corrode, especially where sprinklers hit them with mineral-heavy water. Plastic fixtures turn brittle. Solid brass and copper develop a patina and keep working for two decades or more. The price difference per fixture is real, but spread across a 20-year service life it disappears.</p>
<p>The same logic applies underground. Use direct-burial cable rated for the application, gel-filled waterproof connectors rather than pierce-type clamps, and a transformer with a stainless or powder-coated steel housing mounted at least 12 inches above grade. These details are what a professional design includes by default and what budget kits leave out.</p>

<h2>Guideline 8: Design for the Site, Not the Catalog</h2>
<p>Every Austin property brings a few site conditions that should shape the design.</p>
<h3>Live oaks and cedar elms</h3>
<p>Live oaks have low, spreading canopies and multiple trunks. One uplight rarely does the job. Two or three fixtures placed at the drip line edge, cross-lighting the trunks from different angles, reveal the structure and eliminate the flat look of a single beam. Cedar elms are taller and narrower, well suited to a single 15 to 30 degree spot.</p>
<h3>Limestone and Austin stone</h3>
<p>Grazing, with the fixture set 8 to 12 inches from the base and aimed straight up, pulls every shadow out of chopped limestone. The same technique on smooth stucco shows every trowel mark, so switch to washing from 4 to 6 feet out for flat surfaces.</p>
<h3>Hill Country slopes</h3>
<p>Grade changes mean steps and retaining walls, which mean safety lighting is not optional. Recessed step lights or small downlights under each cap stone should be the first fixtures placed.</p>
<h3>Deer and wildlife</h3>
<p>In neighborhoods with regular deer traffic, choose fixtures on long ground stakes, avoid delicate stems, and keep path light hats low and sturdy. Deer bed down in warm beds and knock things over.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscape-lighting-design-1.webp" alt="Two brass uplights cross-lighting a multi-trunk live oak in an Austin front yard" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Cross-lighting a multi-trunk live oak from two angles reveals its structure and avoids the flat, single-beam look.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Guideline 9: Be a Good Neighbor and Dark-Sky Friendly</h2>
<p>Austin has a long dark-sky tradition, and several Central Texas communities, including Dripping Springs and parts of Travis County, have formal ordinances. Even where rules do not apply, the principles make for better lighting. Aim fixtures down or at a target so no light escapes above horizontal. Use shrouds and glare guards. Keep lumen levels to what the task requires rather than what the fixture can produce. Run the system on an astronomical timer or app control so it shuts off at a reasonable hour rather than burning until dawn.</p>
<p>HOAs across Austin increasingly write these points into their guidelines, and a design that follows them rarely draws a complaint. App-based controls make scheduling and dimming simple enough that most clients run the front yard until 11 p.m. and dim the back to a low security level after that.</p>

